From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kant <283011337+muradkant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 19:59:20 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] Add --cfr: constant frame rate without forced redraws

Live streaming needs a constant frame rate. --no-damage provides one by
asking for every frame with a plain copy, which makes the compositor
redraw the whole output each time, at the full frame rate, even when
nothing on it has changed.

--cfr keeps damage-driven capture and moves the constant rate into the
encoder thread: frames are placed on a real-time grid of slots at -r,
anchored on the first frame, each going to the slot nearest to when it
was rendered. When a slot's window passes without a new frame, the last
converted frame is encoded again, so a still screen costs one encode
per slot and no rendering or conversion at all. Short gaps are filled,
long stalls are skipped, so the output never runs ahead of real time.
Timestamps stay in microseconds and the encoder is given the frame rate
for rate control.
